coder › Done. Here's the summary.

## What I changed

**`src/roman.ts`** — implemented both functions around a single largest-first symbol table (`M, CM, D, CD, C, XC, L, XL, X, IX, V, IV, I`), with each subtractive pair placed before the symbol it subtracts from so the one ordering drives both building and parsing:

- `toRoman(n)` greedily appends symbols while `n >= value`, so `4 → IV`, `9 → IX`, `40 → XL`, `90 → XC`, `400 → CD`, `900 → CM`, `3999 → MMMCMXCIX`. It throws `RangeError` unless `Number.isInteger(n) && 1 ≤ n ≤ 3999` (covers `NaN`, `Infinity`, and fractional inputs).
- `fromRoman(text)` first rejects the empty string and anything not matching the canonical-numeral grammar `^M{0,3}(CM|CD|D?C{0,3})(XC|XL|L?X{0,3})(IX|IV|V?I{0,3})$` (case-insensitive) with a `SyntaxError` that quotes the offending text; it then scans greedily to total the value. This makes the accepted set exactly the canonical numerals, so `IIII`, `VX`, `IC`, `MMMM`, `IM`, etc. are all rejected.

**`src/roman.test.ts`** (new) — uses `bun:test`:
- 12 named `toRoman` cases plus a range/type-error case (`0`, `-1`, `4000`, `1.5`, `NaN`, `±Infinity`).
- A round-trip `fromRoman(toRoman(n)) === n` for every `n` in `1..3999`.
- Case-insensitivity checks.
- Rejection of 10 non-standard strings, and an assertion that the `SyntaxError` message names the offending text.

